1
00:00:00,216 --> 00:00:01,216
[This is a fictional story. All characters
and events bear no relation to reality]

2
00:00:01,216 --> 00:00:02,216
[Child and animal scenes filmed safely]

3
00:00:02,216 --> 00:00:03,394
[under the guardian
and expert supervision per guidelines]

4
00:00:04,383 --> 00:00:07,812
[Episode 8]

5
00:00:07,812 --> 00:00:10,108
I told you I'm all better.

6
00:00:10,108 --> 00:00:13,544
I was cooped up in a hospital for months.
I just needed to get out.

7
00:00:13,544 --> 00:00:17,876
<i>The doctor said you shouldn't
push yourself for a while.</i>

8
00:00:17,876 --> 00:00:20,692
<i>You've only been out for a few days.
Why go somewhere that rough?</i>

9
00:00:20,692 --> 00:00:22,788
Come on, if it were that rough,
I wouldn't have made it here.

10
00:00:22,788 --> 00:00:26,649
The roads are much better these days.
They go all the way up to the lodge...

11
00:00:28,748 --> 00:00:31,624
Oh... Mom, I'm in the mountains,
so the signal is bad.

12
00:00:31,624 --> 00:00:32,787
I'll eat well, get plenty of rest,

13
00:00:32,787 --> 00:00:34,848
and head back in a week,
so don't worry too much.

14
00:00:34,848 --> 00:00:36,548
I'm hanging up now.

15
00:00:43,800 --> 00:00:45,684
Hello! How have you been?

16
00:00:45,684 --> 00:00:47,044
Oh, welcome.

17
00:00:47,044 --> 00:00:48,084
Have you been well?

18
00:00:48,084 --> 00:00:50,432
Oh, careful, it's slippery.

19
00:00:50,432 --> 00:00:51,720
Why are you out in this cold?

20
00:00:51,720 --> 00:00:53,116
You've been well, right?

21
00:00:53,116 --> 00:00:57,480
You usually come toward the end of summer.
What brings you here in the winter?

22
00:00:57,480 --> 00:01:00,888
I had some things to take care of,
so it turned out this way.

23
00:01:00,888 --> 00:01:04,548
The road was rough, so you had
a hard time getting up here, huh?

24
00:01:04,548 --> 00:01:06,452
Yeah, it took a little longer than usual.

25
00:01:06,452 --> 00:01:08,972
Oh, you're lucky the weather's good today.

26
00:01:08,972 --> 00:01:10,749
Once it snows, you can't even get up here.

27
00:01:10,749 --> 00:01:15,683
In the winter, you can only come and go
if the mountain allows it.

28
00:01:15,683 --> 00:01:19,548
You must be tired.
Go on up, unpack, and get some rest.

29
00:01:19,548 --> 00:01:21,096
Okay, it's the same room
I always stay in, right?

30
00:01:21,096 --> 00:01:22,504
No, the room next door.

31
00:01:22,504 --> 00:01:24,048
That one's occupied.

32
00:01:24,048 --> 00:01:25,748
Occupied?

33
00:01:27,564 --> 00:01:30,100
<i>A young woman came a few days ago.</i>

34
00:01:30,100 --> 00:01:32,540
<i>She seems to have no energy.
She just sleeps all day.</i>

35
00:01:32,540 --> 00:01:34,352
<i>She's always collapsing wherever she is.</i>

36
00:01:34,352 --> 00:01:37,149
<i>Oh, I'm worried sick about her.</i>

37
00:02:25,112 --> 00:02:26,812
Excuse...

38
00:02:35,452 --> 00:02:37,516
<i>Doesn't she remember me?</i>

39
00:02:44,016 --> 00:02:45,016
Good morning!

40
00:02:45,016 --> 00:02:47,004
- Oh, welcome!
- Welcome.

41
00:02:47,004 --> 00:02:49,083
Have a seat.

42
00:02:49,083 --> 00:02:50,816
Alright.

43
00:02:54,736 --> 00:02:56,024
Oh, the woman in the next room?

44
00:02:56,024 --> 00:02:59,052
That young lady doesn't
usually eat breakfast.

45
00:02:59,052 --> 00:03:01,044
It would be a relief
if she just skipped breakfast.

46
00:03:01,044 --> 00:03:04,056
She skips lunch and dinner even more often.

47
00:03:04,056 --> 00:03:07,488
Even when she sits down for a meal,
she barely eats a few bites.

48
00:03:07,488 --> 00:03:09,120
How long is she staying?

49
00:03:09,120 --> 00:03:13,924
She paid a month's room
and meal fees up front, but who knows.

50
00:03:13,924 --> 00:03:18,556
There isn't much to do around here.
She might get bored and leave early.

51
00:03:18,556 --> 00:03:21,868
How long are you staying this time?

52
00:03:21,868 --> 00:03:23,983
Well, I was thinking about a week...

53
00:03:26,028 --> 00:03:30,149
I'm thinking of staying
until I get a little bored of it this time.

54
00:03:34,408 --> 00:03:37,349
Oh, come over here and have a bite with us.

55
00:03:39,776 --> 00:03:41,168
Oh, jeez.

56
00:03:41,168 --> 00:03:42,816
Come on, eat.

57
00:03:42,816 --> 00:03:44,416
Okay.

58
00:03:44,416 --> 00:03:49,068
She's so quiet. Looks like
she's heading back into the mountains.

59
00:03:49,068 --> 00:03:51,352
I don't know where she gets
the energy when she barely eats.

60
00:03:51,352 --> 00:03:54,949
Still, it's better than
sleeping in her room all day.

61
00:03:58,924 --> 00:04:01,380
Don't we have too little meat?

62
00:04:01,380 --> 00:04:03,452
Should we grill some meat for dinner?

63
00:04:03,452 --> 00:04:06,116
Yeah, sounds good.

64
00:04:36,540 --> 00:04:38,240
Huh?

65
00:04:41,076 --> 00:04:42,196
Oh...

66
00:04:42,196 --> 00:04:44,992
Wow, you walk incredibly fast.

67
00:04:44,992 --> 00:04:48,492
I thought you were using
some kind of magic shortcut.

68
00:04:48,492 --> 00:04:50,983
I guess you like hiking, huh?

69
00:04:52,764 --> 00:04:54,549
I'm not usually weak in the legs.

70
00:04:54,549 --> 00:04:57,776
It's just that the terrain
around here is pretty rough.

71
00:04:57,776 --> 00:04:59,936
And I had a pretty serious injury recently,

72
00:04:59,936 --> 00:05:02,149
so I spent quite a while in the hospital.

73
00:05:02,149 --> 00:05:05,080
Do you happen to know
Gyeongsan Hospital in Gyeongsan-dong?

74
00:05:05,080 --> 00:05:07,140
Darn it! Ah!

75
00:05:12,408 --> 00:05:16,449
We're having a barbecue tonight.
You have to join us!

76
00:05:18,780 --> 00:05:21,316
Still so aloof.

77
00:05:32,128 --> 00:05:34,716
I see you prefer protein.

78
00:05:35,768 --> 00:05:37,916
Honey, hurry up and grill the meat.
Please grill all of it.

79
00:05:37,916 --> 00:05:42,008
Yeah, we'll have to stock up on meat
next time we go grocery shopping.

80
00:05:49,296 --> 00:05:53,796
By the way, when are you going
to bring your girlfriend?

81
00:05:53,796 --> 00:05:56,388
Don't you get tired of always coming alone?

82
00:05:56,388 --> 00:06:01,349
I haven't met my perfect match
like you two yet.

83
00:06:02,583 --> 00:06:04,356
Honey, the fire's getting low.

84
00:06:04,356 --> 00:06:08,160
I need to get some dry firewood
from the shed.

85
00:06:08,160 --> 00:06:10,128
I'll get it.

86
00:06:10,128 --> 00:06:11,949
Okay, sure.

87
00:06:22,952 --> 00:06:24,949
Give it to me. I'll carry it.

88
00:06:30,016 --> 00:06:32,804
Don't you remember me?
We've met before. Twice.

89
00:06:32,804 --> 00:06:34,640
- Yes, I remember.
- Right?

90
00:06:34,640 --> 00:06:36,516
I knew you'd remember.

91
00:06:36,516 --> 00:06:38,816
Wow, meeting for a third time
all the way out here!

92
00:06:38,816 --> 00:06:41,883
Isn't this an incredible coincidence?

93
00:06:41,883 --> 00:06:44,128
How could something like this happen?

94
00:06:44,128 --> 00:06:46,416
This is such a remote place,
and hardly anyone knows about it.

95
00:06:46,416 --> 00:06:49,312
At this point, I'd say we have
a pretty unusual connection.

96
00:06:49,312 --> 00:06:51,012
Excuse me.

97
00:06:52,720 --> 00:06:53,720
Yes?

98
00:06:53,720 --> 00:06:56,728
I came here
because I don't want to do anything.

99
00:06:56,728 --> 00:06:59,249
- Pardon?
- I just want some peace and quiet.

100
00:07:11,464 --> 00:07:13,449
I'm the one staying in the room next door.

101
00:07:18,592 --> 00:07:22,020
The owners went down
the mountain early this morning.

102
00:07:22,020 --> 00:07:24,149
It's their monthly grocery shopping day.

103
00:07:24,149 --> 00:07:26,420
They asked me to make sure you eat.

104
00:07:26,420 --> 00:07:29,083
But you hadn't come down by lunchtime.

105
00:07:30,068 --> 00:07:32,820
I won't ask you to do anything or bother you.

106
00:07:32,820 --> 00:07:34,649
So let's just eat.

107
00:07:35,268 --> 00:07:38,949
Come down whenever you feel like it.
I'll be waiting.

108
00:09:23,280 --> 00:09:24,983
Excuse me!

109
00:09:26,388 --> 00:09:29,283
I think you should come outside for a moment.

110
00:09:30,296 --> 00:09:31,996
Excuse me?

111
00:09:36,640 --> 00:09:40,083
I just got a call from the owner.

112
00:09:40,083 --> 00:09:43,324
All roads leading into the mountain
have been closed since yesterday afternoon.

113
00:09:43,324 --> 00:09:46,392
And they've just imposed
an indefinite ban on entry.

114
00:09:46,392 --> 00:09:48,549
Looks like we're stranded.

115
00:10:19,632 --> 00:10:21,312
Where have you been?

116
00:10:21,312 --> 00:10:23,083
Put some clothes on and come out.

117
00:10:44,528 --> 00:10:47,783
So this is where they store
all the food supplies.

118
00:10:50,792 --> 00:10:52,424
But how did you find...

119
00:10:52,424 --> 00:10:54,249
I found out when I came to get some firewood.

120
00:10:54,249 --> 00:10:59,044
Let's take the things that'll spoil quickly
or can't be eaten once frozen.

121
00:10:59,044 --> 00:11:00,749
Oh, okay.

122
00:11:02,236 --> 00:11:04,244
There isn't much left here either.

123
00:11:04,244 --> 00:11:05,391
Still, if we ration it,

124
00:11:05,391 --> 00:11:07,300
the two of us should
be able to make it for about a week, right?

125
00:11:07,300 --> 00:11:09,372
The roads will be open by then, right?

126
00:11:09,372 --> 00:11:11,316
It could take longer.

127
00:11:18,812 --> 00:11:20,532
Oh, oh!

128
00:11:20,532 --> 00:11:21,668
It's okay.

129
00:11:21,668 --> 00:11:24,816
Oh, this is for hunting.
That startled you, right?

130
00:11:24,816 --> 00:11:29,004
Lodges this deep in the mountains
usually keep one of these around.

131
00:11:29,004 --> 00:11:30,849
Because of wild animals.

132
00:11:35,088 --> 00:11:37,416
Oh, don't touch that carelessly...

133
00:11:38,828 --> 00:11:40,528
It's dangerous.

134
00:11:56,824 --> 00:11:58,908
Wait, those are bullets?

135
00:11:58,908 --> 00:12:00,851
That's...

136
00:12:00,851 --> 00:12:03,249
Why are you taking those...

137
00:12:06,772 --> 00:12:09,783
Huh? Why are you... Why are you taking them?

138
00:12:21,572 --> 00:12:25,384
Is there anything making
you uncomfortable here...

139
00:12:25,384 --> 00:12:26,696
Like what?

140
00:12:26,696 --> 00:12:29,736
I mean, since it's just the two of us here,

141
00:12:29,736 --> 00:12:33,136
you might feel a little uneasy as a woman.

142
00:12:33,136 --> 00:12:34,836
Not really.

143
00:12:36,340 --> 00:12:38,040
That's a relief.

144
00:12:40,348 --> 00:12:42,416
What's your name?

145
00:12:43,296 --> 00:12:46,408
Oh, I'm Kwon Tae Seong, Kwon Tae Seong.

146
00:12:46,408 --> 00:12:50,583
If you're uncomfortable, you can just
give me a fake name or a nickname.

147
00:12:50,583 --> 00:12:53,548
I just need something to call you.

148
00:12:53,548 --> 00:12:56,383
It's Bo Na, Yoo Bo Na.

149
00:12:57,100 --> 00:12:58,952
Oh, Yoo Bo Na?

150
00:12:58,952 --> 00:13:00,652
Yoo...

151
00:13:11,108 --> 00:13:14,428
The pipes aren't going to freeze, are they?

152
00:13:14,428 --> 00:13:16,749
We can just leave the water running a little.

153
00:13:19,280 --> 00:13:23,320
Or the roof collapsing...
from all the snow...

154
00:13:23,320 --> 00:13:25,449
It won't collapse from this much snow.

155
00:13:28,412 --> 00:13:30,216
The gas could get cut off too.

156
00:13:30,216 --> 00:13:31,660
There's a portable stove in the shed.

157
00:13:31,660 --> 00:13:33,360
I see.

158
00:13:34,760 --> 00:13:37,492
What should we make for dinner?

159
00:13:37,492 --> 00:13:40,983
Do you happen to know how to cook?

160
00:14:02,852 --> 00:14:05,284
We should add some soybean paste, right?

161
00:14:05,284 --> 00:14:07,320
I suppose so.

162
00:14:07,320 --> 00:14:09,583
But how much...

163
00:14:10,196 --> 00:14:11,896
Is that too little?

164
00:14:14,616 --> 00:14:16,140
[For stock]
We should add some anchovies, right?

165
00:14:16,140 --> 00:14:17,840
I guess so.

166
00:14:18,776 --> 00:14:19,804
Two?

167
00:14:19,804 --> 00:14:21,096
Two?

168
00:14:21,096 --> 00:14:22,764
- Two?
- Isn't that too many?

169
00:14:22,764 --> 00:14:24,716
At least one each...

170
00:14:26,492 --> 00:14:28,192
Yeah.

171
00:14:39,056 --> 00:14:40,756
What's the problem...

172
00:15:24,600 --> 00:15:26,088
You go ahead.

173
00:15:26,088 --> 00:15:28,083
No, you go ahead first...

174
00:15:50,920 --> 00:15:52,620
Good night.

175
00:16:57,024 --> 00:17:01,236
I'm not usually the type
to get scared easily.

176
00:17:01,236 --> 00:17:03,464
But I've never experienced

177
00:17:03,464 --> 00:17:08,016
a snowstorm this severe,
with such strong winds, in my life.

178
00:17:11,124 --> 00:17:14,428
The power's out, the boiler's down,

179
00:17:14,428 --> 00:17:16,683
there's no phone service,

180
00:17:18,248 --> 00:17:20,660
and we're running out of food...

181
00:17:20,660 --> 00:17:23,349
We're basically refugees, aren't we?

182
00:17:27,756 --> 00:17:30,744
And the snow just keeps coming down.

183
00:17:30,744 --> 00:17:33,016
It'll stop tomorrow.

184
00:17:35,876 --> 00:17:37,716
How do you know that...

185
00:17:49,483 --> 00:17:52,216
Then I'll head back to my room.

186
00:17:52,216 --> 00:17:54,349
Sleep here with me.

187
00:17:56,164 --> 00:17:59,476
You'll freeze to death
sleeping alone in that room.

188
00:17:59,476 --> 00:18:02,249
This is the warmest spot, anyway.

189
00:18:16,976 --> 00:18:19,581
What's the first thing you want to do

190
00:18:19,581 --> 00:18:22,149
when you leave the mountain, Ms. Yoo Bo Na?

191
00:18:24,916 --> 00:18:29,484
I mean, once you go back to your normal life?

192
00:18:29,484 --> 00:18:31,624
Well... nothing, really.

193
00:18:31,624 --> 00:18:33,049
There's nothing you want to do?

194
00:18:33,049 --> 00:18:35,884
Or anyone you want to see?

195
00:18:35,884 --> 00:18:38,149
No, nothing.

196
00:18:39,896 --> 00:18:41,245
<i>Huh?</i>

197
00:18:41,245 --> 00:18:44,049
I came here
because I don't want to do anything.

198
00:18:44,049 --> 00:18:45,983
<i>I just want some peace and quiet.</i>

199
00:19:33,688 --> 00:19:36,752
<i>♪ We wish you a Merry Christmas ♪</i>

200
00:19:36,752 --> 00:19:39,472
<i>♪ We wish you a Merry Christmas ♪</i>

201
00:19:39,472 --> 00:19:42,496
<i>♪ We wish you a Merry Christmas ♪</i>

202
00:19:42,496 --> 00:19:46,083
<i>♪ And a happy New Year ♪</i>

203
00:19:46,812 --> 00:19:49,652
Well, our situation may not be ideal,

204
00:19:49,652 --> 00:19:53,544
but just like you predicted,
the snow has stopped.

205
00:19:53,544 --> 00:19:56,264
And it's Christmas today.

206
00:19:56,264 --> 00:19:58,249
Merry Christmas!

207
00:19:58,900 --> 00:20:00,600
You didn't know, right?

208
00:20:02,324 --> 00:20:08,049
Oh, this is a Christmas present
for you, Bo Na!

209
00:20:32,284 --> 00:20:34,216
Bo Na... Bo Na.

210
00:20:34,836 --> 00:20:39,449
Do you see that little bird
peeking out between the branches?

211
00:20:40,480 --> 00:20:43,049
That wouldn't even be a single bite.

212
00:20:47,856 --> 00:20:50,964
Isn't it tiny and adorable?
So cute and round?

213
00:20:50,964 --> 00:20:54,796
Oh, it's incredible how it survives
so bravely in weather this cold.

214
00:20:54,796 --> 00:20:56,583
Pretty amazing, huh?

215
00:21:14,984 --> 00:21:17,044
Bo Na.

216
00:21:17,044 --> 00:21:19,716
Come over here and look.

217
00:21:21,232 --> 00:21:24,660
What do you think of
when you see icicles, Bo Na?

218
00:21:24,660 --> 00:21:27,783
It'd be perfect for piercing
a vital spot in one go.

219
00:21:33,312 --> 00:21:35,916
Aren't they beautiful and crystal clear?

220
00:21:36,884 --> 00:21:40,276
When I was little, I used to suck on them.

221
00:21:40,276 --> 00:21:42,916
We could make shaved ice with them, right?

222
00:21:52,660 --> 00:21:54,360
Bo Na!

223
00:21:55,804 --> 00:21:57,740
Ta-da!

224
00:21:57,740 --> 00:22:00,144
I found this stuck in the firewood.

225
00:22:00,144 --> 00:22:01,924
Isn't it incredibly romantic?

226
00:22:01,924 --> 00:22:05,349
It was around this time of year
when I first met you.

227
00:22:08,486 --> 00:22:10,716
Can you hold out your hand for a second?

228
00:22:21,368 --> 00:22:23,583
I'll see if I can find another one.

229
00:22:37,060 --> 00:22:39,516
I think the sweet potatoes are almost done.

230
00:22:44,992 --> 00:22:46,983
It's so atmospheric, isn't it?

231
00:22:54,144 --> 00:22:56,783
It would've been nice to have some meat too.

232
00:23:02,216 --> 00:23:05,524
I actually came here to report a story once.

233
00:23:05,524 --> 00:23:07,949
Oh, I'm a reporter.

234
00:23:11,424 --> 00:23:13,124
Anyway,

235
00:23:14,688 --> 00:23:17,464
I parked my car down the mountain
and started walking up.

236
00:23:17,464 --> 00:23:18,639
I must've taken a wrong turn,

237
00:23:18,639 --> 00:23:22,049
and no matter how far I walked,
I couldn't find my way.

238
00:23:22,049 --> 00:23:24,568
I was hungry. My legs were aching.

239
00:23:24,568 --> 00:23:28,328
I thought, "I can't go any farther,"
and just sat down.

240
00:23:28,328 --> 00:23:34,244
But then, I casually turned my head,
and right in front of me,

241
00:23:34,244 --> 00:23:38,016
there was a field of wildflowers.

242
00:23:38,016 --> 00:23:39,308
Like some hidden paradise,

243
00:23:39,308 --> 00:23:44,268
a huge field of flowers spread out
in a place I'd never have expected.

244
00:23:44,268 --> 00:23:47,500
That was the first time
I'd ever felt my heart drop

245
00:23:47,500 --> 00:23:50,292
at the sight of something so beautiful.

246
00:23:50,292 --> 00:23:52,516
As I gazed quietly at the field of flowers,

247
00:23:52,516 --> 00:23:56,016
my heart grew more generous,
and my mood lifted.

248
00:23:57,008 --> 00:24:00,632
Like every cell in my body
was coming back to life, you know?

249
00:24:00,632 --> 00:24:04,768
Thanks to that, I was able to get
my energy back and start moving again.

250
00:24:04,768 --> 00:24:08,049
Oh, and of course, I finished
my reporting without a hitch.

251
00:24:08,840 --> 00:24:10,969
I think that's the power of a beautiful view.

252
00:24:10,969 --> 00:24:13,580
My own little views that give me energy

253
00:24:13,580 --> 00:24:18,392
when I'm tired and help me
get moving again when I stop.

254
00:24:18,392 --> 00:24:21,804
For some people, it could be something

255
00:24:21,804 --> 00:24:27,860
as ordinary as flowers, sunsets,
or stars in the night sky.

256
00:24:27,860 --> 00:24:31,749
For someone else,
it might be a particular moment.

257
00:24:33,628 --> 00:24:36,216
Or even a person.

258
00:24:38,912 --> 00:24:42,683
Have you ever had a beautiful moment
like that in your life, Bo Na?

259
00:24:46,688 --> 00:24:48,828
No.

260
00:24:48,828 --> 00:24:50,649
You haven't? Not even once?

261
00:24:50,649 --> 00:24:54,149
Because I've never thought of
anything as beautiful.

262
00:24:58,083 --> 00:25:00,516
From now on, I'll show you plenty.

263
00:25:05,916 --> 00:25:07,440
I mean...

264
00:25:07,440 --> 00:25:12,716
It's nighttime here
and too dark to see anything pretty.

265
00:25:21,208 --> 00:25:23,616
But there is one beautiful sight.

266
00:25:41,840 --> 00:25:44,383
Oh, the sweet potatoes are going to burn.

267
00:26:05,792 --> 00:26:07,516
Your face is getting burnt.

268
00:26:55,228 --> 00:26:56,928
Why is the gun...

269
00:27:03,004 --> 00:27:04,704
Ms. Yoo Bo Na!

270
00:27:12,384 --> 00:27:14,084
Bo Na!

271
00:27:17,236 --> 00:27:18,936
Bo Na!

272
00:27:26,772 --> 00:27:29,183
Bo Na! Where are you?

273
00:27:42,548 --> 00:27:44,483
Bo Na!

274
00:27:45,552 --> 00:27:47,604
Bo Na!

275
00:27:47,604 --> 00:27:49,516
Bo Na!

276
00:27:52,636 --> 00:27:54,516
Bo Na!

277
00:27:56,384 --> 00:27:58,505
Bo Na!

278
00:27:58,505 --> 00:28:00,383
Bo Na!

279
00:28:07,752 --> 00:28:09,452
Bo Na!

280
00:28:11,121 --> 00:28:12,849
Bo Na!

281
00:28:56,284 --> 00:28:59,016
Why is he lying here like this...

282
00:29:03,224 --> 00:29:05,516
I can't even call an ambulance in this place.

283
00:29:30,583 --> 00:29:34,816
[If I Ever Meet Kingfisher]

284
00:29:36,448 --> 00:29:39,068
<i>As time passed, I became a reporter.</i>

285
00:29:39,068 --> 00:29:40,768
[Spring, 2017]
Mr. Oh!

286
00:29:40,768 --> 00:29:43,616
Do you admit to stock manipulation,
embezzlement, and breach of trust?

287
00:29:43,616 --> 00:29:45,680
There are hundreds of victims!

288
00:29:45,680 --> 00:29:46,916
Don't you feel sorry?

289
00:29:46,916 --> 00:29:52,244
<i>I've watched countless offenders
escape proper punishment.</i>

290
00:29:52,244 --> 00:29:53,412
Mr. Oh!

291
00:29:53,412 --> 00:29:55,583
<i>I've spent countless days feeling despair</i>

292
00:29:55,583 --> 00:29:57,543
<i>as I faced the walls in our society that</i>

293
00:29:57,543 --> 00:29:58,883
<i>no amount of effort
seemed able to break through.</i>

294
00:29:58,883 --> 00:30:00,200
[Kingfisher's Target: A Child Sex Abuser]
<i>The gunman known as Kingfisher</i>

295
00:30:00,200 --> 00:30:01,200
<i>has committed another murder...</i>

296
00:30:01,200 --> 00:30:02,306
<i>But even during that time,</i>

297
00:30:02,306 --> 00:30:04,520
[Kingfisher Victim Exposed For]
<i>Kingfisher continued his work.</i>

298
00:30:04,520 --> 00:30:07,249
[Exploiting Disabled Workers]
<i>Many people tried to figure out who he was</i>

299
00:30:07,249 --> 00:30:09,516
[A Week Later, Kingfisher Strikes Again]
<i>and speculated on why he did such things.</i>

300
00:30:09,516 --> 00:30:10,648
[Victim Was a Gangster]
<i>But I didn't.</i>

301
00:30:10,648 --> 00:30:12,244
<i>Was found shot.</i>

302
00:30:12,244 --> 00:30:15,389
<i>- I never carelessly speculated about him.
- The weight is on the fact</i>

303
00:30:15,389 --> 00:30:17,349
[Rental Scam Real Estate CEO Found Shot Dead]
<i>that it was Kingfisher's crime.</i>

304
00:30:18,728 --> 00:30:21,704
<i>So even if Kingfisher's identity
is revealed someday,</i>

305
00:30:21,704 --> 00:30:23,876
<i>I won't be surprised.</i>

306
00:30:23,876 --> 00:30:26,249
<i>Even if he's someone
who defies all expectations,</i>

307
00:30:26,249 --> 00:30:29,032
<i>like a white-haired old man,</i>

308
00:30:29,032 --> 00:30:33,156
<i>I'll simply look forward to the day
I finally meet him.</i>

309
00:30:33,156 --> 00:30:36,664
<i>I want to sit beside him by the campfire</i>

310
00:30:36,664 --> 00:30:39,700
<i>and listen to him talk about
his life all night.</i>

311
00:30:39,700 --> 00:30:42,728
<i>Like two strangers who met while traveling,</i>

312
00:30:42,728 --> 00:30:46,116
<i>or old friends seeing each other again
after a long time.</i>

313
00:30:47,040 --> 00:30:50,900
<i>Without asking anything,
I could simply listen,</i>

314
00:30:50,900 --> 00:30:55,683
<i>with a warm cup of coffee or tea by my side.</i>

315
00:31:35,896 --> 00:31:37,883
You're awake?

316
00:31:48,940 --> 00:31:51,156
I was so scared.

317
00:31:51,156 --> 00:31:53,316
I was afraid you might disappear.

318
00:31:53,976 --> 00:31:57,083
I'm so relieved, really relieved,
that you came back safe.

319
00:32:05,192 --> 00:32:07,583
Thank you for coming back.

320
00:32:44,192 --> 00:32:46,649
What's this? Where did you get the meat...

321
00:32:47,360 --> 00:32:49,808
I found some in the freezer.

322
00:32:49,808 --> 00:32:53,116
Was there chicken? How did I not see it?

323
00:32:56,944 --> 00:32:58,644
Thank you for the meal.

324
00:33:19,532 --> 00:33:21,684
This is so good.

325
00:33:21,684 --> 00:33:25,349
I think this is the best chicken porridge
I've ever had.

326
00:33:29,608 --> 00:33:32,416
But do you always cry that easily?

327
00:33:36,364 --> 00:33:38,064
Pardon?

328
00:33:41,028 --> 00:33:42,728
No, I don't.

329
00:33:47,516 --> 00:33:50,683
It's actually the first time I've cried
since I graduated from elementary school.

330
00:33:58,784 --> 00:34:00,649
It's a first for me too.

331
00:34:02,316 --> 00:34:04,436
What is?

332
00:34:04,436 --> 00:34:07,249
Someone crying because
they were afraid I'd disappear.

333
00:34:09,132 --> 00:34:11,783
I didn't come here to die.

334
00:34:13,100 --> 00:34:15,816
I've never even thought about wanting to die.

335
00:34:19,868 --> 00:34:22,849
I really... I didn't know you thought that.

336
00:34:23,536 --> 00:34:24,592
Then what about the gun?

337
00:34:24,592 --> 00:34:27,316
I just put it back where it was.

338
00:34:31,176 --> 00:34:33,249
Then what about the gunshot?

339
00:34:34,736 --> 00:34:37,583
It seems like there might be a hunter around.

340
00:34:38,368 --> 00:34:42,452
Right, how did I get here...

341
00:34:42,452 --> 00:34:45,652
Did you carry me here on your back...

342
00:34:45,652 --> 00:34:47,949
I rolled you here.

343
00:34:50,416 --> 00:34:52,116
What?

344
00:34:53,372 --> 00:34:55,783
We're out of water. I'll get you some water.

345
00:35:05,449 --> 00:35:08,116
[Seasoned Salt]

346
00:35:44,360 --> 00:35:46,583
Bo Na, here!

347
00:35:51,224 --> 00:35:54,549
This is the wildflower field
I told you about.

348
00:35:57,200 --> 00:36:01,116
It's my first time seeing the landscape
look like this when it snows too.

349
00:36:04,516 --> 00:36:06,588
I wish there had been flowers here.

350
00:36:06,588 --> 00:36:08,288
But it's winter,

351
00:36:09,384 --> 00:36:11,449
and snow flowers are flowers too.

352
00:36:32,012 --> 00:36:34,249
I like you, Bo Na.

353
00:36:41,700 --> 00:36:44,216
You don't really know much about me, do you?

354
00:36:44,216 --> 00:36:47,116
You're right. I don't know much about you.

355
00:36:47,752 --> 00:36:49,783
I know your name, but...

356
00:36:50,960 --> 00:36:52,983
I don't even know if it's your real name.

357
00:36:52,983 --> 00:36:55,849
I don't know where you were born,
how you grew up,

358
00:36:56,840 --> 00:37:00,536
what your family is like,
or what you do for a living.

359
00:37:00,536 --> 00:37:05,283
I don't know why you were
standing there that day

360
00:37:06,220 --> 00:37:08,056
when we first met.

361
00:37:08,056 --> 00:37:10,683
But I do know some sides of you, Bo Na.

362
00:37:10,683 --> 00:37:12,816
You climb rough mountain paths
without hesitation.

363
00:37:12,816 --> 00:37:15,380
You prefer meat over vegetables.

364
00:37:15,380 --> 00:37:20,024
And in a crisis, you're calmer
and stronger than anyone.

365
00:37:20,024 --> 00:37:21,724
And...

366
00:37:23,480 --> 00:37:27,880
you make the most delicious
chicken porridge in the world.

367
00:37:27,880 --> 00:37:29,876
Perhaps love begins

368
00:37:29,876 --> 00:37:31,956
when you find yourself drawn to

369
00:37:31,956 --> 00:37:35,449
just one side of a person
among all the many sides they have.

370
00:37:37,356 --> 00:37:39,616
I've already fallen in love.

371
00:38:01,204 --> 00:38:03,983
My name is Yoo Bo Na. It's my real name.

372
00:38:05,160 --> 00:38:07,464
Please take care of me.

373
00:38:07,464 --> 00:38:09,516
It's my first time with love.

374
00:38:32,004 --> 00:38:33,749
It's beautiful.

375
00:39:11,149 --> 00:39:15,692
My mom and my older sister
run a chicken shop together.

376
00:39:15,692 --> 00:39:17,772
Do you know what it is called?

377
00:39:17,772 --> 00:39:19,549
Tae Seong Chicken?

378
00:39:22,504 --> 00:39:27,600
My second oldest sister
works at an insurance company.

379
00:39:27,600 --> 00:39:29,500
And the youngest is Se Ah...

380
00:39:29,500 --> 00:39:35,183
So that woman on the rooftop was your sister.

381
00:39:36,360 --> 00:39:38,383
Oh, yes, that's right.

382
00:39:51,152 --> 00:39:54,096
Maybe it's because it's spring,
but you look healthier.

383
00:39:54,096 --> 00:39:56,316
Did you have a good vacation?

384
00:39:57,096 --> 00:39:58,796
Yes.

385
00:40:01,412 --> 00:40:05,116
Have you made up your mind?

386
00:40:14,292 --> 00:40:15,992
My resignation letter...

387
00:40:17,540 --> 00:40:19,588
Please throw it away.

388
00:40:19,588 --> 00:40:21,716
Please forget I ever submitted it.

389
00:40:28,604 --> 00:40:30,304
Alright.

390
00:40:32,348 --> 00:40:34,449
What made you come back?

391
00:40:40,660 --> 00:40:43,016
I saw a beautiful landscape.

392
00:40:43,744 --> 00:40:45,444
What?

393
00:40:50,616 --> 00:40:55,316
The ocean really is the best place
to clear your head, after all.

394
00:41:07,004 --> 00:41:09,252
- Hey, is it good?
- What do you want?

395
00:41:09,252 --> 00:41:11,396
- I asked if it's good.
- Go away.

396
00:41:11,396 --> 00:41:13,249
I said go away!

397
00:41:14,032 --> 00:41:17,092
Go away, hurry, go!

398
00:41:17,092 --> 00:41:19,264
Ma'am, ma'am!

399
00:41:19,264 --> 00:41:20,648
I'll push it for you.

400
00:41:20,648 --> 00:41:22,583
- Huh?
- Who are you?

401
00:41:33,916 --> 00:41:36,383
[Not Today]

402
00:41:36,383 --> 00:41:37,720
[Social Affairs Desk]

403
00:41:37,720 --> 00:41:39,836
Hey.

404
00:41:39,836 --> 00:41:41,896
- Why are you wearing a suit?
- I don't know, man.

405
00:41:41,896 --> 00:41:43,596
You came early?

406
00:41:55,832 --> 00:41:57,228
What's this?

407
00:41:57,228 --> 00:42:02,683
It's to thank you for giving me the chance
to prove myself with my reporting.

408
00:42:04,404 --> 00:42:06,849
Thank you. I'll enjoy it.

409
00:42:07,944 --> 00:42:10,724
I'm about to be completely broke.

410
00:42:10,724 --> 00:42:12,424
Why?

411
00:42:14,832 --> 00:42:15,832
Thank you!

412
00:42:15,832 --> 00:42:17,532
Oh, jeez.

413
00:42:20,196 --> 00:42:21,364
Seriously...

414
00:42:21,364 --> 00:42:25,100
I've also decided to buy
a huge team dinner soon!

415
00:42:25,100 --> 00:42:28,416
A round of applause for our Hae Na!

416
00:42:37,208 --> 00:42:41,368
I made some egg rice this morning,
but I made way too much.

417
00:42:41,368 --> 00:42:43,448
I guess I tend to make too much.

418
00:42:43,448 --> 00:42:45,148
Here.

419
00:42:46,576 --> 00:42:48,460
Are you sure it's safe to eat?

420
00:42:48,460 --> 00:42:50,668
Don't eat it.

421
00:42:50,668 --> 00:42:52,416
I'll just eat it and die.

422
00:43:01,308 --> 00:43:03,616
Sometimes I can't believe it myself, either.

423
00:43:07,192 --> 00:43:10,580
I still can't believe I can cook like this.

424
00:43:10,580 --> 00:43:15,104
Jeez, you should've come to the housewarming.
There was a lot of good food.

425
00:43:15,104 --> 00:43:16,476
I'd just be a burden.

426
00:43:16,476 --> 00:43:18,076
Oh, dear.

427
00:43:18,076 --> 00:43:21,508
Hey, you seriously need to
cut down on instant food.

428
00:43:21,508 --> 00:43:23,312
You'll ruin your health.

429
00:43:23,312 --> 00:43:25,483
Is it good to have a family?

430
00:43:27,296 --> 00:43:28,568
Yeah, it's good.

431
00:43:28,568 --> 00:43:30,449
More than friends?

432
00:43:33,484 --> 00:43:35,316
Friends are...

433
00:43:36,276 --> 00:43:40,449
like guests who sometimes come to the island
where I live, bringing good food?

434
00:43:41,392 --> 00:43:43,116
Family is...

435
00:43:43,844 --> 00:43:47,392
the very island I'm standing on.

436
00:43:47,392 --> 00:43:50,883
My one and only island...

437
00:43:50,883 --> 00:43:53,749
in this endless sea, I guess?

438
00:43:54,683 --> 00:43:56,516
My little piece of land.

439
00:43:58,188 --> 00:44:00,892
You've got something
to protect now, Yoo Bo Na.

440
00:44:00,892 --> 00:44:02,752
You sound just like someone I know.

441
00:44:02,752 --> 00:44:04,452
Huh?

442
00:44:04,452 --> 00:44:06,383
That face of yours.

443
00:44:12,420 --> 00:44:13,960
Do you like it?

444
00:44:13,960 --> 00:44:16,004
It's made from a new lightweight material.

445
00:44:16,004 --> 00:44:17,983
Yeah, I like it.

446
00:44:20,728 --> 00:44:23,216
Can I borrow this for the weekend?

447
00:44:35,604 --> 00:44:38,400
If you're that curious,
why don't you go inside?

448
00:44:38,400 --> 00:44:40,700
What do you mean? I'm not curious.

449
00:44:40,700 --> 00:44:44,192
Those two are friends. They've known
each other since they were kids.

450
00:44:44,192 --> 00:44:48,849
Our brilliant Director Yoo,
and a filthy, smelly shut-in?

451
00:44:52,328 --> 00:44:53,864
What am I, by any chance?

452
00:44:53,864 --> 00:44:56,783
A sickly, obnoxious keyboard jockey.

453
00:44:56,783 --> 00:44:59,449
Jeez, I can't believe this.

454
00:45:04,736 --> 00:45:06,668
You forgot to take this.

455
00:45:06,668 --> 00:45:10,416
Se Ah put a lot of effort into making it.
Wouldn't it be rude not to take it?

456
00:45:16,000 --> 00:45:19,040
Man, a man really needs
to be good-looking, after all.

457
00:45:19,040 --> 00:45:22,388
So this is what they mean by
good looks getting you places.

458
00:45:22,388 --> 00:45:24,576
I know exactly how he feels.

459
00:45:24,576 --> 00:45:27,708
I was exactly like that when I was young.

460
00:45:27,708 --> 00:45:29,449
When?

461
00:45:30,149 --> 00:45:31,440
When I was young.

462
00:45:31,440 --> 00:45:34,449
That girl's got quite a taste...

463
00:45:38,608 --> 00:45:39,685
- It's good.
- Honey, I'll do it.

464
00:45:39,685 --> 00:45:40,820
You didn't even have lunch.

465
00:45:40,820 --> 00:45:42,728
- You need to eat more.
- I'm eating.

466
00:45:42,728 --> 00:45:43,964
- Eat the fish first.
- Okay, I will.

467
00:45:43,964 --> 00:45:45,640
Your favorite fish.

468
00:45:45,640 --> 00:45:47,124
Does your brother not have hands?

469
00:45:47,124 --> 00:45:50,316
The day you return my clothes,
let's go on a date.

470
00:45:59,332 --> 00:46:01,284
You crazy punk...

471
00:46:01,284 --> 00:46:03,683
Why did I even ask her that?

472
00:46:12,272 --> 00:46:14,036
What should I do?

473
00:46:18,848 --> 00:46:20,144
I don't have his number.

474
00:46:20,144 --> 00:46:21,536
Should I ask Bo Na?

475
00:46:21,536 --> 00:46:24,760
Then she'd probably tell me
to bring it to her office.

476
00:46:24,760 --> 00:46:28,783
Then what am I supposed to do about my date?

477
00:46:35,104 --> 00:46:38,316
Yes, Your Majesty. What is the matter?

478
00:46:39,464 --> 00:46:40,688
Jeez, Mom!

479
00:46:40,688 --> 00:46:42,883
I told you not to take orders
from that place.

480
00:46:42,883 --> 00:46:45,683
Why do you keep sending me
all the way over there...

481
00:46:48,100 --> 00:46:51,880
Mom, using highly skilled talent
like me for errands like this

482
00:46:51,880 --> 00:46:55,416
is a waste of national resources!

483
00:47:02,716 --> 00:47:05,883
[Tae Seong Chicken]

484
00:47:16,149 --> 00:47:19,424
[I'm here]

485
00:47:19,424 --> 00:47:21,412
Get off, get off!

486
00:47:21,412 --> 00:47:23,420
Darn it, I'm going!

487
00:47:23,420 --> 00:47:24,748
Let go!

488
00:47:24,748 --> 00:47:26,476
Darn it.

489
00:47:29,092 --> 00:47:30,836
Oh...

490
00:47:34,336 --> 00:47:37,583
The punk came in guilty,
and now he's laughing?

491
00:47:39,672 --> 00:47:42,256
Oh, Se Dol! You got here
sooner than I expected.

492
00:47:42,256 --> 00:47:44,064
Hey, you want to die?

493
00:47:44,064 --> 00:47:46,183
Don't you have a chicken shop
in your neighborhood?

494
00:47:46,183 --> 00:47:47,183
Yeah, we don't.

495
00:47:47,183 --> 00:47:48,901
This neighborhood's nothing but
pizza places and snack bars.

496
00:47:48,901 --> 00:47:51,184
Then eat pizza or snacks around here.

497
00:47:51,184 --> 00:47:53,416
Why are you boosting the sales of
a chicken shop in another neighborhood?

498
00:47:53,416 --> 00:47:55,516
Don't you have any respect
for the local economy?

499
00:47:55,516 --> 00:47:57,283
You brought plenty of
radish and salad, right?

500
00:47:57,283 --> 00:48:01,248
Keep ordering, and you might end up
getting fried with the chicken.

501
00:48:01,248 --> 00:48:02,400
Hey, just wear your helmet properly.

502
00:48:02,400 --> 00:48:04,124
Don't get slapped with a fine for no reason.

503
00:48:04,124 --> 00:48:05,316
I'm going.

504
00:48:05,316 --> 00:48:07,016
Go.

505
00:48:11,780 --> 00:48:13,748
What? I'm busy.

506
00:48:13,748 --> 00:48:17,660
From now on, answer my questions
short and sweet.

507
00:48:17,660 --> 00:48:18,920
Short and sweet about what?

508
00:48:18,920 --> 00:48:21,194
Say you're going on a first date
with some woman.

509
00:48:21,194 --> 00:48:22,480
- What?
- A first date.

510
00:48:22,480 --> 00:48:25,584
- With whom?
- Jeez, I'm just giving you an example.

511
00:48:25,584 --> 00:48:27,344
A hypothetical woman.

512
00:48:27,344 --> 00:48:29,780
- And?
- You ask her,

513
00:48:29,780 --> 00:48:32,216
"What would you like to do?"

514
00:48:32,960 --> 00:48:35,020
What do you mean? We have to eat.

515
00:48:35,020 --> 00:48:37,448
You jump straight to food
the second you meet?

516
00:48:37,448 --> 00:48:39,496
Then what do you do?

517
00:48:39,496 --> 00:48:40,876
Fine. Let's say you have a meal with her.

518
00:48:40,876 --> 00:48:45,240
Then if you say, "Let's get something
to eat first. What would you like?"

519
00:48:45,240 --> 00:48:47,826
What would she say that
you'd find attractive?

520
00:48:47,826 --> 00:48:50,549
Chicken feet! You love those.

521
00:48:51,804 --> 00:48:53,049
I do.

522
00:48:53,049 --> 00:48:57,872
But for a first date,
chicken feet might be a little...

523
00:48:57,872 --> 00:49:01,421
Then your second pick,
all-you-can-eat frozen pork belly?

524
00:49:01,421 --> 00:49:03,148
Jeez, forget the food.

525
00:49:03,148 --> 00:49:04,796
What about the hair?

526
00:49:04,796 --> 00:49:06,496
Bangs down?

527
00:49:08,160 --> 00:49:09,224
Or pushed back?

528
00:49:09,224 --> 00:49:11,540
Definitely bangs down.
You inevitably need to cover up a lot.

529
00:49:11,540 --> 00:49:14,349
Jeez, forget it. Just go eat your chicken.

530
00:49:14,349 --> 00:49:16,083
Jeez.

531
00:49:19,020 --> 00:49:20,949
This isn't about me, you know?

532
00:49:25,292 --> 00:49:26,992
What is she saying?

533
00:49:29,604 --> 00:49:31,304
Hold on.

534
00:49:34,549 --> 00:49:36,120
[If you keep thinking about someone all day,]

535
00:49:36,120 --> 00:49:38,408
[and smile every time they cross your mind,]
Wow, is this for real?

536
00:49:38,408 --> 00:49:41,383
[is that love?]
I'm the only guy around her.

537
00:49:41,383 --> 00:49:42,383
Could it be me?

538
00:49:42,383 --> 00:49:43,513
What do you mean, you?

539
00:49:43,513 --> 00:49:45,228
What? No, it's nothing.

540
00:49:45,228 --> 00:49:46,484
Come into the interrogation room.

541
00:49:46,484 --> 00:49:47,667
Why?

542
00:49:47,667 --> 00:49:48,700
It's about Hwang Cheol Beom.

543
00:49:48,700 --> 00:49:50,400
Now?

544
00:49:52,316 --> 00:49:54,228
Sir, please go ahead and eat this first.

545
00:49:54,228 --> 00:49:55,983
I'm heading to the interrogation room...

546
00:49:56,664 --> 00:49:57,852
Please don't eat my chicken.

547
00:49:57,852 --> 00:49:59,649
- Alright.
- Yeah.

548
00:50:01,183 --> 00:50:03,032
[Tae Seong Chicken]

549
00:50:03,032 --> 00:50:04,716
Ugh, Tae Seong Chicken again!

550
00:50:04,716 --> 00:50:05,816
"Why did you take so long?"

551
00:50:05,816 --> 00:50:07,883
"I reported this ages ago."

552
00:50:09,280 --> 00:50:11,149
"Why aren't you cuffing me already?"

553
00:50:13,312 --> 00:50:16,216
Apparently, he kept saying things like that.

554
00:50:17,312 --> 00:50:18,708
Mr. Hwang Cheol Beom,

555
00:50:18,708 --> 00:50:20,749
why did you do that
right after getting released?

556
00:50:20,749 --> 00:50:22,892
I don't know. Lost my temper?

557
00:50:22,892 --> 00:50:26,404
Oh, so you beat up random people
just because you were angry?

558
00:50:26,404 --> 00:50:30,216
Aggravated assault and bodily injury
will almost certainly get you prison time.

559
00:50:30,216 --> 00:50:34,688
And the sentence will be even harsher
when there are multiple random victims.

560
00:50:34,688 --> 00:50:38,036
Plus, with your prior violent offense,

561
00:50:38,036 --> 00:50:40,349
the repeat-offender enhancement
could keep you locked up for a long time.

562
00:50:40,349 --> 00:50:42,834
You just got out.
You really want to go back in already?

563
00:50:42,834 --> 00:50:44,468
Oh, it's fine.

564
00:50:44,468 --> 00:50:47,449
It's more comfortable in there than out here.

565
00:50:48,221 --> 00:50:51,432
Afraid you'll get shot if you go outside?

566
00:50:51,432 --> 00:50:55,108
You brutally killed your sister-in-law
when she resisted your attempted rape.

567
00:50:55,108 --> 00:50:56,508
You deserved life in prison,

568
00:50:56,508 --> 00:51:00,183
but you fed them that "sudden impulse"
and "mentally weak" nonsense

569
00:51:01,514 --> 00:51:03,716
and got just 15 years.

570
00:51:03,716 --> 00:51:05,520
You're exactly the kind of person
Kingfisher would target, aren't you?

571
00:51:05,520 --> 00:51:09,349
Yeah, I did that because I was afraid of
getting shot and dying. So what?

572
00:51:12,076 --> 00:51:15,480
Do you know what it's like
in prison right now?

573
00:51:15,480 --> 00:51:18,104
Everyone's anxious and making a fuss.

574
00:51:18,104 --> 00:51:20,256
Some guys who've done bad things
but got lighter sentences

575
00:51:20,256 --> 00:51:22,400
are causing trouble inside on purpose

576
00:51:22,400 --> 00:51:25,283
because they're afraid Kingfisher
will kill them once they're released.

577
00:51:25,283 --> 00:51:28,076
Better to stay inside
than to live in anxiety,

578
00:51:28,076 --> 00:51:31,183
shaking at the thought of bullets
flying from who knows where.

579
00:51:31,183 --> 00:51:34,720
Oh, a lot of the guys who got out before me

580
00:51:34,720 --> 00:51:36,629
have gone missing too.

581
00:51:36,629 --> 00:51:38,976
Are you saying some of them were shot dead?

582
00:51:38,976 --> 00:51:40,124
How would I know?

583
00:51:40,124 --> 00:51:42,083
Whether they died from getting shot,
or got hit by a car,

584
00:51:42,083 --> 00:51:44,524
or fell and died...

585
00:51:44,524 --> 00:51:47,521
And for all we know,
Kingfisher doesn't even always

586
00:51:47,521 --> 00:51:50,105
use a gun, right?

587
00:51:50,105 --> 00:51:55,049
Either way, everyone's saying
it's all Kingfisher's doing.

588
00:51:57,276 --> 00:52:02,483
And people can make mistakes in life, right?

589
00:52:02,483 --> 00:52:04,316
Hey, you punk.

590
00:52:05,367 --> 00:52:08,024
What you did wasn't a mistake.

591
00:52:08,024 --> 00:52:10,244
What?

592
00:52:10,244 --> 00:52:14,016
We spent taxpayers' money giving you
a chance to repent and rehabilitate,

593
00:52:14,016 --> 00:52:16,964
and this is what you do
the second you get out?

594
00:52:16,964 --> 00:52:18,340
And what about you people?

595
00:52:18,340 --> 00:52:21,083
Isn't all this happening because the police
can't catch Kingfisher?

596
00:52:21,083 --> 00:52:23,844
Is that why you only targeted
the elderly and women?

597
00:52:23,844 --> 00:52:26,481
Too scared to mess with younger men?

598
00:52:26,481 --> 00:52:29,088
You're just a pathetic coward
who can only prey on the weak!

599
00:52:29,088 --> 00:52:31,349
How dare you run your mouth!

600
00:52:34,048 --> 00:52:37,060
Jeez, he killed innocent people
and calls it a mistake?

601
00:52:37,060 --> 00:52:40,048
You being born is a mistake, you punk!

602
00:52:40,048 --> 00:52:41,933
Hey, Beom.

603
00:52:41,933 --> 00:52:43,675
Pick 20 recently released convicts

604
00:52:43,675 --> 00:52:46,156
who you think got light sentences
for the severity of their crimes,

605
00:52:46,156 --> 00:52:49,528
and find out what's happened to them
since they got out. Hurry.

606
00:52:49,528 --> 00:52:51,544
What? Why?

607
00:52:51,544 --> 00:52:53,008
Oh, you mean guys like him?

608
00:52:53,008 --> 00:52:54,871
Yes, understood.

609
00:52:54,871 --> 00:52:57,472
Did you look into that dead body
at the villa in Hyeonsa-dong?

610
00:52:57,472 --> 00:53:00,496
Oh, the guy who died with his face
in a bowl of jjajangmyeon?

611
00:53:00,496 --> 00:53:02,908
They didn't find anything in the autopsy.

612
00:53:02,908 --> 00:53:05,075
They checked his medical records
for any underlying condition,

613
00:53:05,075 --> 00:53:07,049
but they said there was nothing notable.

614
00:53:08,683 --> 00:53:09,683
Alright.

615
00:53:09,683 --> 00:53:10,826
Finish Hwang Cheol Beom's report

616
00:53:10,826 --> 00:53:12,924
and transfer the case
to the prosecution by tomorrow.

617
00:53:12,924 --> 00:53:14,512
I'm leaving.

618
00:53:14,512 --> 00:53:15,888
Where are you going?

619
00:53:15,888 --> 00:53:17,932
- The supermarket.
- What?

620
00:53:17,932 --> 00:53:19,796
The supermarket?

621
00:53:19,796 --> 00:53:24,183
Oh, boy, now I can finally sleep soundly.

622
00:53:29,449 --> 00:53:32,149
Oh, my legs.

623
00:53:34,496 --> 00:53:36,575
Good grief.

624
00:53:36,575 --> 00:53:42,749
All that scheming just to survive...

625
00:53:43,708 --> 00:53:47,540
But should prison

626
00:53:47,540 --> 00:53:51,352
be a hideout for criminals?

627
00:53:51,352 --> 00:53:56,649
We're already overcrowded as it is,
running out of space,

628
00:53:57,852 --> 00:53:59,704
aren't we?

629
00:53:59,704 --> 00:54:04,216
<i>♪ Dari-dari-da ♪</i>

630
00:54:50,472 --> 00:54:52,172
You're late.

631
00:54:53,420 --> 00:54:55,672
Why am I so bad at this
after not doing it for so long?

632
00:54:55,672 --> 00:54:59,004
How do you remember every year without fail?
Do you have an alarm set?

633
00:54:59,004 --> 00:55:01,016
Open the door. My legs hurt.

634
00:55:01,671 --> 00:55:03,348
Anyone would think you were the son.

635
00:55:03,348 --> 00:55:05,116
Then am I the daughter?
Did you get everything?

636
00:55:05,116 --> 00:55:06,916
I bought it all.

637
00:55:08,283 --> 00:55:11,783
[Lee Pil Ho, Ju Hye Gyeong]

638
00:55:30,149 --> 00:55:33,349
[Lee Pil Ho, Ju Hye Gyeong]

639
00:55:56,432 --> 00:55:58,640
It's almost been 20 years.

640
00:55:58,640 --> 00:56:01,983
Wow. Has it really been that long?

641
00:56:02,800 --> 00:56:05,948
Do you still dream about
what happened that day?

642
00:56:05,948 --> 00:56:08,988
Maybe it's because the ancestral rite
is coming up...

643
00:56:08,988 --> 00:56:10,783
I dreamed about it last night too.

644
00:56:16,968 --> 00:56:18,668
Have a drink.

645
00:56:23,456 --> 00:56:26,488
You talked me into becoming a reporter.

646
00:56:26,488 --> 00:56:28,508
And now that I think about it again,
it pisses me off.

647
00:56:28,508 --> 00:56:30,692
You bring that up every chance you get.

648
00:56:30,692 --> 00:56:32,792
Hey, do you think I wanted to?

649
00:56:32,792 --> 00:56:35,832
You made a huge fuss
about catching the culprit.

650
00:56:35,832 --> 00:56:37,472
And in the end, you couldn't catch him.

651
00:56:37,472 --> 00:56:40,128
Hey, but at least you became a cop, right?

652
00:56:40,128 --> 00:56:42,883
Oh, man, for real.

653
00:56:47,760 --> 00:56:49,768
Are the police in this country
really competent?

654
00:56:49,768 --> 00:56:54,116
If they're that competent, why did it
take them four years to catch him?

655
00:56:58,336 --> 00:57:01,760
That punk still hasn't been released, right?

656
00:57:01,760 --> 00:57:04,483
Not yet. Next year.

657
00:57:06,204 --> 00:57:07,988
Today...

658
00:57:07,988 --> 00:57:11,692
I saw a punk just like him.

659
00:57:11,692 --> 00:57:13,480
That punk said the same thing.

660
00:57:13,480 --> 00:57:16,592
That it wasn't intentional.
That it was a mistake.

661
00:57:16,592 --> 00:57:18,949
I wonder if he's still the same.

662
00:57:18,949 --> 00:57:22,683
No remorse, no rehabilitation.

663
00:57:24,676 --> 00:57:26,916
Has he changed at all?

664
00:57:28,652 --> 00:57:30,356
Pretty. Are these hyacinths?

665
00:57:30,356 --> 00:57:32,816
Yeah, that's right.

666
00:57:34,536 --> 00:57:35,548
Ma'am!

667
00:57:35,548 --> 00:57:38,049
- Yeah.
- I've finished picking out the flowers.

668
00:57:38,049 --> 00:57:39,548
Oh, they're lovely!

669
00:57:39,548 --> 00:57:42,856
Who did our Yul pick such pretty flowers for?

670
00:57:42,856 --> 00:57:45,256
- It's a secret!
- Huh? My goodness.

671
00:57:45,256 --> 00:57:47,116
She's all grown up now,
knowing about secrets.

672
00:57:47,116 --> 00:57:48,972
It feels like just yesterday
she could barely say a word.

673
00:57:48,972 --> 00:57:52,152
Now she often tries to outsmart me.
I can't handle her.

674
00:57:52,152 --> 00:57:53,552
Oh, dear.

675
00:57:53,552 --> 00:57:56,524
Well, even the mighty Yoo Bo Na
has someone she can't beat.

676
00:57:56,524 --> 00:58:00,128
Well, that's what kids are like.

677
00:58:00,128 --> 00:58:02,912
I don't know if I'm doing a good job.

678
00:58:02,912 --> 00:58:04,524
What do you mean?

679
00:58:04,524 --> 00:58:09,349
The fact that you're worrying about that
is proof you're doing a good job.

680
00:58:10,468 --> 00:58:12,440
Now that I think about it, Yoo Bo Na,

681
00:58:12,440 --> 00:58:15,216
it reminds me of when you first had Yul.

682
00:58:16,664 --> 00:58:18,983
And the things you said back then too.

683
00:58:22,052 --> 00:58:23,416
Yul, no! Don't do that!

684
00:58:23,416 --> 00:58:25,328
Oh, dear, Yul, Yul!

685
00:58:25,328 --> 00:58:27,892
No, don't.

686
00:58:27,892 --> 00:58:31,249
Ma'am, what's the room over there?

687
00:58:32,216 --> 00:58:35,083
That's my secret too.

688
00:58:37,884 --> 00:58:39,849
Tell me the truth.

689
00:58:40,592 --> 00:58:42,683
You're a member...

690
00:58:44,172 --> 00:58:46,072
of the Kingfisher fan club, aren't you?

691
00:58:46,072 --> 00:58:48,584
What are you talking about, you crazy punk?

692
00:58:48,584 --> 00:58:54,236
You were obsessed with digging
into Kingfisher until a few years ago.

693
00:58:54,236 --> 00:58:56,068
You were constantly hounding me about it.

694
00:58:56,068 --> 00:58:59,480
Then one day, you just stopped.

695
00:58:59,480 --> 00:59:01,312
Why? Why all of a sudden? Why?

696
00:59:01,312 --> 00:59:03,176
Come on, be honest with me. What? Why?

697
00:59:03,176 --> 00:59:06,300
You're a Kingfisher fan, aren't you?

698
00:59:06,300 --> 00:59:07,863
Hey, enough nonsense.

699
00:59:07,863 --> 00:59:10,560
There's something
I've honestly wondered about for a long time.

700
00:59:10,560 --> 00:59:15,083
Why are you so obsessed with
catching Kingfisher?

701
00:59:16,412 --> 00:59:19,348
You punk, you must be the Kingfisher fan...

702
00:59:19,348 --> 00:59:21,449
Fan club president, right?

703
00:59:21,449 --> 00:59:23,424
- Hey, hey!
- King...

704
00:59:23,424 --> 00:59:27,228
I'm a cop!

705
00:59:27,228 --> 00:59:30,488
Yeah, you're a cop. So what?

706
00:59:30,488 --> 00:59:32,912
You're the family of a crime victim.

707
00:59:32,912 --> 00:59:34,772
I know. I know that too.

708
00:59:34,772 --> 00:59:37,236
Oh, Kingfisher?

709
00:59:37,236 --> 00:59:38,396
He's cool.

710
00:59:38,396 --> 00:59:41,344
Like some hero who takes down evil.

711
00:59:41,344 --> 00:59:45,248
Like a grim reaper taking revenge for me.

712
00:59:45,248 --> 00:59:48,760
But there's a side of him people don't know.

713
00:59:48,760 --> 00:59:52,449
A punk who shot a dad dead

714
00:59:53,808 --> 00:59:57,264
right in front of his seven-year-old son.

715
00:59:57,264 --> 01:00:00,628
Four years ago, at the scene of
Kingfisher's last known crime.

716
01:00:00,628 --> 01:00:05,908
The kid was so traumatized
that he still can't speak.

717
01:00:05,908 --> 01:00:07,616
Jeez.

718
01:00:07,616 --> 01:00:08,976
Hey.

719
01:00:08,976 --> 01:00:14,084
A normal human being
wouldn't kill a parent in front of a child.

720
01:00:14,084 --> 01:00:18,516
No matter how vile the criminal was,
that still isn't right, right?

721
01:00:20,036 --> 01:00:21,286
<i>No matter what anyone says,</i>

722
01:00:21,286 --> 01:00:22,652
- <i>to me, Kingfisher is...</i>
- Yul, is it pretty?

723
01:00:22,652 --> 01:00:24,308
Yeah!

724
01:00:24,308 --> 01:00:30,516
<i>a cold-blooded,
heartless psychopath and murderer.</i>

725
01:00:30,516 --> 01:00:32,683
I feel like I'm going to throw up.

726
01:00:33,676 --> 01:00:36,649
- Huh?
- I seriously feel like throwing up.

727
01:00:39,549 --> 01:00:41,883
Aim properly!

728
01:00:43,996 --> 01:00:46,749
Otherwise, I'll have to clean it up.

729
01:00:47,896 --> 01:00:52,016
If he were just a psychopath
who killed anyone,

730
01:00:52,016 --> 01:00:55,983
it'd be easier to call him pure evil.

731
01:00:57,183 --> 01:01:04,060
But that punk only kills criminals,

732
01:01:04,060 --> 01:01:09,084
so people can't decide
whether he's good or evil.

733
01:01:09,084 --> 01:01:14,372
He taps into the dark side

734
01:01:14,372 --> 01:01:18,548
hidden deep inside everyone.

735
01:01:18,548 --> 01:01:20,783
What a bad punk!

736
01:01:28,168 --> 01:01:29,964
I have something to confess.

737
01:01:29,964 --> 01:01:32,116
You want to die?

738
01:01:33,036 --> 01:01:34,588
Get your hands off me.

739
01:01:34,588 --> 01:01:36,288
I...

740
01:01:38,752 --> 01:01:40,516
I know.

741
01:01:41,316 --> 01:01:44,716
I have a gun at the station, you know?

742
01:01:45,496 --> 01:01:51,676
I... know who Kingfisher is.

743
01:01:51,676 --> 01:01:53,376
What?

744
01:02:09,484 --> 01:02:11,216
Reporter Kwon Tae Seong.

745
01:02:11,824 --> 01:02:16,508
Forget everything that happened today.

746
01:02:16,508 --> 01:02:21,549
And from now on, you'd better
stop looking into Kingfisher.

747
01:02:25,816 --> 01:02:28,249
If you break your promise...

748
01:02:49,736 --> 01:02:51,720
Honey, why are you up so early?

749
01:02:51,720 --> 01:02:53,449
Huh? Well...

750
01:02:53,449 --> 01:02:54,584
Daddy!

751
01:02:54,584 --> 01:02:56,612
Oh, Yul!

752
01:02:56,612 --> 01:02:59,212
Were you playing with Kkongsoon?

753
01:02:59,212 --> 01:03:00,976
Oh, Daddy, you smell like alcohol.

754
01:03:00,976 --> 01:03:02,192
Oh, put me down.

755
01:03:02,192 --> 01:03:03,504
I'm sorry.

756
01:03:03,504 --> 01:03:06,016
I'll brush my teeth and hug you later.

757
01:03:09,776 --> 01:03:12,604
How much did you drink
with Dong Jin last night?

758
01:03:12,604 --> 01:03:14,152
Ugh, I don't know.

759
01:03:14,152 --> 01:03:17,472
He suddenly went on a drinking binge.
What time did I get home last night?

760
01:03:17,472 --> 01:03:18,904
Around 2 AM?

761
01:03:18,904 --> 01:03:22,524
You came home in a taxi, showered,
changed, and went straight to bed.

762
01:03:22,524 --> 01:03:24,172
And you don't remember any of it?

763
01:03:24,172 --> 01:03:25,920
Wow. I must've completely blacked out.

764
01:03:25,920 --> 01:03:27,460
I don't remember any of it.

765
01:03:27,460 --> 01:03:29,024
- Really?
- Yeah.

766
01:03:29,024 --> 01:03:30,148
Really?

767
01:03:30,148 --> 01:03:31,640
Are you going somewhere?

768
01:03:31,640 --> 01:03:34,224
Yeah, I have something
to take care of real quick.

769
01:03:34,224 --> 01:03:36,624
Yul, come eat. Hurry.

770
01:03:36,624 --> 01:03:38,880
It's the weekend.

771
01:03:38,880 --> 01:03:40,620
I'll be back soon.

772
01:03:40,620 --> 01:03:43,436
You can look after Yul while I'm gone, right?

773
01:03:43,436 --> 01:03:44,436
Of course.

774
01:03:44,436 --> 01:03:47,544
I'm still pretty hungover,
but I can handle it.

775
01:03:47,544 --> 01:03:50,268
Honey, you really reek of alcohol.
Go brush your teeth. Hurry.

776
01:03:50,268 --> 01:03:51,788
Sorry.

777
01:03:51,788 --> 01:03:53,892
Yul!

778
01:03:53,892 --> 01:03:55,880
Yul!

779
01:03:55,880 --> 01:03:57,852
That's right.

780
01:03:57,852 --> 01:04:00,049
All right, pick up your spoon.

781
01:04:27,660 --> 01:04:29,376
<i>Hi, Bo Na.</i>

782
01:04:29,376 --> 01:04:31,788
<i>Are your Achilles' heels doing well?</i>

783
01:04:31,788 --> 01:04:33,028
<i>I miss you.</i>

784
01:04:33,028 --> 01:04:34,804
<i>I'll come to you.</i>

785
01:04:34,804 --> 01:04:40,212
<i>I hope I finally get to meet them this time.</i>

786
01:04:40,212 --> 01:04:41,916
<i>From your friend.</i>

787
01:04:45,352 --> 01:04:49,083
I'm sure I talked about
something important...

788
01:04:51,384 --> 01:04:55,883
Daddy, why did you color the rainbow black?

789
01:04:56,496 --> 01:04:59,252
Oh, sorry.

790
01:04:59,252 --> 01:05:01,100
Stop!

791
01:05:01,100 --> 01:05:03,349
<i>I have something to confess.</i>

792
01:05:03,349 --> 01:05:05,716
Actually, I...

793
01:05:05,716 --> 01:05:07,416
I know.

794
01:05:08,608 --> 01:05:10,816
I know...

795
01:05:10,816 --> 01:05:13,283
who Kingfisher is.

796
01:05:34,612 --> 01:05:37,592
<i>Hey, what?</i>

797
01:05:37,592 --> 01:05:40,280
Go home and sleep.

798
01:05:40,280 --> 01:05:42,712
I saw him. I really did.

799
01:05:42,712 --> 01:05:44,976
Saw what? Who?

800
01:05:44,976 --> 01:05:47,756
Kingfisher!

801
01:05:47,756 --> 01:05:49,400
Kingfisher!

802
01:05:49,400 --> 01:05:50,564
Kingfisher?

803
01:05:50,564 --> 01:05:51,752
Yeah!

804
01:05:51,752 --> 01:05:53,549
Really?

805
01:05:53,549 --> 01:05:55,280
Lucky you.

806
01:05:55,280 --> 01:05:58,580
Hey, what does he look like?

807
01:05:58,580 --> 01:06:00,280
Kingfisher?

808
01:06:08,249 --> 01:06:11,383
[Seul Gi]

809
01:06:17,080 --> 01:06:19,176
<i>Kingfisher is...</i>

810
01:06:19,176 --> 01:06:23,796
<i>a tall, well-built</i>

811
01:06:23,796 --> 01:06:25,880
<i>young man.</i>

812
01:06:25,880 --> 01:06:27,580
Who are you?

813
01:06:33,972 --> 01:06:35,756
Oh, Bo Na!

814
01:06:35,756 --> 01:06:37,000
Where are you right now?

815
01:06:37,000 --> 01:06:39,184
Well...

816
01:06:39,184 --> 01:06:41,249
Guess where I am.

817
01:06:41,916 --> 01:06:43,836
Do you know my wife?

818
01:06:43,836 --> 01:06:46,883
<i>What's your relationship with Bo Na...</i>

819
01:06:46,883 --> 01:06:49,880
<i>Nice to meet you, Mr. Kwon Tae Seong.</i>

820
01:06:49,880 --> 01:06:53,749
I'm Sergei Volansky, a friend of Yoo Bo Na.

821
01:06:53,749 --> 01:06:55,156
Oh, well...

822
01:06:55,156 --> 01:06:57,416
<i>My Korean name is...</i>

823
01:06:57,416 --> 01:06:59,376
Seul Gi.

824
01:06:59,376 --> 01:07:01,360
I'm Seul Gi.

825
01:07:01,360 --> 01:07:03,716
<i>If you break your promise,</i>

826
01:07:06,260 --> 01:07:10,216
your wife and your child,

827
01:07:10,216 --> 01:07:12,949
your family could be in danger.

828
01:07:51,916 --> 01:07:55,956
You seem to know very little
about Bo Na, Mr. Kwon Tae Seong.

829
01:07:55,956 --> 01:07:58,924
I already heard it,
and I can't pretend I haven't.

830
01:07:58,924 --> 01:08:01,008
I have someone I need to protect too.

831
01:08:01,008 --> 01:08:03,649
So those two are your Achilles' heels?

832
01:08:05,072 --> 01:08:06,840
I told you not to show your face
in front of me again, didn't I?

833
01:08:06,840 --> 01:08:09,428
Because you'll end up
betraying me first anyway.

834
01:08:09,428 --> 01:08:12,480
These are the violent offenders
singled out from this year's releases.

835
01:08:12,480 --> 01:08:14,864
Dong Jin, that punk's been released.

836
01:08:14,864 --> 01:08:17,328
He already got a ridiculously light sentence,
and now he's even been paroled...

837
01:08:17,328 --> 01:08:19,476
Do you think he might do something dangerous?

838
01:08:19,476 --> 01:08:22,375
Make it cool, but it won't be easy.

839
01:08:22,375 --> 01:08:24,716
Goodbye, Tae Seong.
